<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Discover — targum</title>
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}{{ asset('chrome.css') }}{{ asset('words.css') }}{{ asset('library.css') }}</style>
<script>{{ asset('theme.js') }}</script>
</head>
<body class="words shelf">
{% set here = 'library' %}{% set title = 'Discover' %}
{% include '_nav.html.j2' %}

<main>
  {#- One list, not two. What the reader has built and what is waiting to be built are
      the same question — what shall I read — and splitting them into a shelf and a
      catalogue meant answering it twice, in two different shapes. -#}
  {#- Two halves of one question, and they are not a filter: the catalogue is everybody's
      and the uploads are yours alone. As a select called "Access" among five other
      controls, the reader's own texts were a setting nobody found. -#}
  <div class="tabs" id="where" role="tablist" aria-label="Which texts"></div>

  <section class="panel" id="picked">
    <p class="note" id="picked-note">Tap a text to read it.</p>

    {#- The controls are the page. A library of a hundred texts is only as good as the
        speed of getting to the one you want, so they sit above the list and act on it
        as you type. -#}
    <div class="sift">
      <input
        id="find" class="find" type="search" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
        placeholder="Search a title or an author" aria-label="Search the library">
      {#- Biblical and modern Hebrew are not two genres, they are close to two languages,
          and which one somebody is learning is the first question this page asks. A
          switch with its own label rather than a third row of pills: as pills it sat
          beside the kind filter with a second chip also saying "All", and the two read
          as one row of ten. -#}
      <div class="switch-set">
        <span class="switch-label">Hebrew</span>
        <div class="segmented" id="register-chips" role="group" aria-label="Which Hebrew"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chips" id="kind-chips" role="group" aria-label="What it is"></div>
      <label class="pick"><span>Length</span><select id="length"></select></label>
      <label class="pick"><span>Difficulty</span><select id="difficulty"></select></label>
      <button type="button" class="clear" id="clear" hidden>Clear</button>
    </div>

    <div class="rows-head" id="rows-head" role="row"></div>
    <ul class="rows" id="catalogue"></ul>
    <p class="empty" id="picked-empty" hidden></p>
    <p class="tally" id="tally"></p>
  </section>

  {#- Somebody who has just failed to find what they wanted is exactly who needs the
      add page. A link, not a form: this page's job is browsing, and a pricing-and-
      progress flow living inside it would have made both jobs worse. -#}
  <p class="elsewhere">Not here? <a href="/add">Add a text of your own</a>.</p>
</main>
{% include '_foot.html.j2' %}

<script>
window.TARGUM_KEY = "{{ token }}";
window.TARGUM_LANGUAGES = { {% for code, name in languages %}"{{ code }}": "{{ name }}"{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} };
window.TARGUM_CATALOGUE = {{ catalogue|tojson }};
</script>
<script>{{ asset('lang.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('sync.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('account.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('covers.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('library.js') }}</script>
</body>
</html>
